			<div class="content" id="post-content-4534132">
									

<p>I'm honestly not a fan of the repeated use of &quot;subhuman&quot; in this article, especially when in the description there's better way of describing the object: &quot;human subspecies&quot;. The word &quot;subhuman&quot; carries inherent pejorative connotation imo, and even if this is literal flying cannibal monster I find it unlikely that an official scientific document would use the word to describe it.</p>
<p>I also would argue against classifying it as subspecies of <em>Homo sapiens.</em> The morphological similarity is kinda diminished &#8212; the object's wings, lack of lower body and leg, etc make it seems less like a subspecies of <em>Homo sapiens</em> and more of an independent species entirely. Maybe even up to different order, since the wings are the defining feature of Chiroptera order. There's no mention of sexual cross-reproduction capability between normal human and this species; indeed, the species is mentioned as incapable of reproducing sexually, with its reproduction by consuming human subjects more reminiscent of tarantula hawks' parasitic larvae. Compare to relationship between <em>Homo sapiens sapiens</em> and <em>Homo sapiens neanderthalis</em>, for example, which resulted in <em>neanderthalis</em> gene existing in a significant portion of current <em>sapiens</em> population; signifying the fertility of their offsprings. This is another arguments against classifying this species as <em>Homo sapiens</em> since one of the requirements for classifying a species is capability to sexually produce fertile offspring.</p>

							</div>

								
					
